feat(verify): recover a rewritten commit's original from a note (#294)

VER-003 promises the original attribution of a rewritten commit stays
resolvable via Rekor. Rekor is keyed on the hash of the original commit SHA,
and the verifier found that SHA only by walking the object database for a
commit with the same tree. A clone of a rewritten branch never holds the
originals, so the promise held only on the machine that did the rewrite.

A rewrite can now leave each original object as a git note on the commit
that replaced it, under refs/notes/innsegl/original. The verifier trusts the
note for nothing: git hashes its bytes as a commit object, only that hash is
asked of the log, the note's tree must be this commit's tree, and the
identity is read from the log entry. The verdict is unchanged: a rewritten
commit stays failed, as VER-003 requires.

A clone does not fetch notes by default, so the report says how when it
finds nothing.

Measured on a rewritten copy of main with 144 notes, from a clone holding
none of the originals: every original that verifies today is recovered.

Driven by VER-021 and VER-022 in internal/verify.

